## Proof

Let $P = A + B$ and $Q = A - B$. Then
$$A^2 - B^2 = \tfrac{1}{2}(PQ + QP),$$
so $A^2 = B^2$ is equivalent to $PQ + QP = 0$, i.e. **$P$ and $Q$ anticommute**: $PQ = -QP$.

Set $C := AB - BA$. A direct computation gives
$$C = AB - BA = \tfrac{1}{2}(QP - PQ) = -PQ = QP,$$
using $QP = -PQ$. Note $BA - AB = -C$, so we must find invertible $S$ with $SC = -CS$, i.e. **$S$ anticommutes with $C$**.

**Key observation.** $P$ anticommutes with $C$:
$$CP = (QP)P = QP^2, \qquad PC = P(QP) = (PQ)P = (-QP)P = -QP^2 = -CP.$$
Likewise $Q$ anticommutes with $C$:
$$CQ = (QP)Q = Q(PQ) = Q(-QP) = -Q^2P, \qquad QC = Q(QP) = Q^2P = -CQ.$$

If $P$ (or $Q$) is invertible we are done immediately by taking $S = P$ (or $S = Q$). We now handle the general case by showing $C$ is similar to $-C$.

**Generalized eigenspaces.** From $CP = -PC$ we get $(C - \lambda I)P = -P(C + \lambda I)$, hence $(C-\lambda I)^k P = (-1)^k P(C+\lambda I)^k$. Thus $P$ maps the generalized eigenspace $G_{-\lambda} := \ker(C+\lambda I)^n$ into $G_\lambda := \ker(C-\lambda I)^n$.

For $\lambda \neq 0$: if $v \in G_\lambda$ and $Pv = 0$, then $Cv = QPv = 0$, but $C|_{G_\lambda}$ has sole eigenvalue $\lambda \neq 0$, forcing $v = 0$. So $P: G_\lambda \to G_{-\lambda}$ is injective; by symmetry it is an isomorphism. Moreover the relation $(C+\lambda I)P = -P(C-\lambda I)$ shows $P$ intertwines the nilpotent operator $(C-\lambda I)|_{G_\lambda}$ with $-(C+\lambda I)|_{G_{-\lambda}}$, so the Jordan block structure of $C$ at $\lambda$ equals that at $-\lambda$.

**Zero eigenvalue.** On $G_0 = \ker C^n$, the operator $C$ is nilpotent. Any nilpotent matrix is similar to its negative: for a Jordan block $J_k(0)$, conjugating by $D = \operatorname{diag}(1,-1,1,-1,\dots)$ gives $D\,J_k(0)\,D^{-1} = -J_k(0)$. Hence $C|_{G_0}$ is similar to $-C|_{G_0}$.

**Conclusion.** Combining both parts: the Jordan form of $C$ at every eigenvalue $\lambda$ matches the Jordan form of $-C$ at $-\lambda$ (nonzero $\lambda$ by the $P$-intertwining; $\lambda = 0$ by nilpotence). Therefore $C$ and $-C$ have identical Jordan forms, so there exists an invertible matrix $S$ with
$$SCS^{-1} = -C, \qquad\text{i.e.}\qquad S(AB - BA) = (BA - AB)\,S.$$

